Accessibility + Frontend audit — Islamic Kids UI
Date: 2026-09-03
Scope: UI/styles.css, surah.css, stories.css, story-styles.css, interactive-story.css,
       bilingual.js, surah.js, stories.js, surah-fatiha.html, story-prophet-*.html
Method: read-only. Contrast computed via WCAG relative-luminance formula in /tmp/a11y.py
        (sRGB -> linear -> 0.2126R+0.7152G+0.0722B; ratio = (L1+.05)/(L2+.05)).
        Hardcoded-hex census via regex over the four page-type sheets + styles.css.
No server/browser was needed; all findings are static-analysis + code-read, file:line cited.

================================================================
CRITICAL
================================================================

C1. Quranic Arabic verse text fails contrast badly (1.86:1, needs 3:1 for large text)
    UI/surah.css:459 `.arabic-full { color: var(--gold-light); }` — used inside `.verse-card`,
    whose background is `#FFFDF8` (UI/surah.css:297, near-white). --gold-light resolves to
    #E0B75C (UI/styles.css:17).
    Computed: gold-light(#E0B75C) on verse-card(#FFFDF8) = 1.86:1. FAILS AA even at the large-text
    (3:1) threshold — this is the worst pair on the site. It renders on every surah page
    (`<p class="arabic-full" dir="rtl">`, e.g. UI/surah-fatiha.html:212) and is exactly the core
    Quran text the owner reads with his son. It also fails hardest on print/grayscale/faded ink,
    which matters here per the stated print-to-read-aloud use case.
    Same variable at UI/surah.css:264 `.arabic-large` is fine — that one sits on `.bismillah-card`
    whose background is `--ink` (#14383A): gold-light on ink = 6.70:1, PASS. So --gold-light is a
    correct choice on dark cards and a contrast failure on light cards — it is being reused across
    two backgrounds with opposite requirements.
    Fix: on `.verse-card` (light bg) use `--gold-text` (#8A6220, 5.06:1 on parchment) or `--ink`,
    not `--gold-light`. Keep `--gold-light` only on dark/`--ink` backgrounds.

C2. Hero eyebrow gold fails AA even as large text
    UI/styles.css:229 `.hero-eyebrow { font-size: 2.5rem; color: var(--gold); }` on `.hero`
    background = `--parchment` (#FBF6EA) (UI/styles.css:171-179, radial gradient overlay is
    ~7% alpha and doesn't materially change luminance).
    Computed: gold(#C08A2E) on parchment(#FBF6EA) = 2.82:1. Font is 40px (qualifies as "large
    text", 3:1 threshold) and still fails. This is the homepage-style hero eyebrow — high
    visibility, high failure.
    Fix: swap to `--gold-text` (#8A6220 → 5.06:1) or add a dark text-shadow/outline; do not use
    raw `--gold` as a foreground color on parchment at any size.

C3. surah.js hides verse/fact/tip cards with an inline `opacity:0` that has no CSS fallback —
    real invisibility risk, worse than "known issue" framing suggests for this project's actual
    use case (printing pages to read aloud)
    UI/surah.js:187-198 (`initializeAnimations`):
        document.querySelectorAll('.verse-card, .fact-card, .tip-card').forEach(card => {
            card.style.opacity = '0';               // <- inline style, JS-set
            card.style.transform = 'translateY(30px)';
            ...
            observer.observe(card);
        });
    The observer only flips opacity back to 1 for cards that have scrolled into the viewport
    (`entries.forEach(... entry.isIntersecting ...)`, UI/surah.js:178-184). Cards below the fold
    that never intersect stay `opacity:0` forever.
    Verified this is NOT mitigated by the site's `prefers-reduced-motion` block
    (UI/styles.css:834-846): that block only forces `.fade-in { opacity: 1 !important; }` and
    speeds up animation/transition durations — it never targets `.verse-card/.fact-card/.tip-card`,
    which get their opacity from a plain (non-!important) inline style, not the `.fade-in` class.
    So a user with reduced-motion turned on still gets invisible cards if the observer never fires.
    Concrete failure modes, ranked by likelihood for this project:
      - Ctrl+P / browser Print without first scrolling through the page: most cards below the
        fold never intersected, so they print blank. This is the scenario the owner described
        (printing a surah page to read to his son) — HIGH real risk.
      - JS disabled or blocked (privacy extensions, content blockers): cards never get opacity
        set back to 1 at all — permanently blank, worse than "no animation," it's no content.
      - Very old/embedded browsers without IntersectionObserver: same as above, and there's no
        `if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window))` feature-detect fallback anywhere in surah.js.
      - Full-page screenshot tools that don't scroll (many do scroll-and-stitch, so this is a
        secondary risk vs. print).
    Fix (cheap and safe): move the opacity/transform into a CSS class (`.reveal-pending`) applied
    via JS instead of inline styles, and add `@media print { .verse-card, .fact-card, .tip-card,
    .reveal-pending { opacity: 1 !important; transform: none !important; } }` plus a
    `<noscript>` rule or a `.reveal-pending { opacity: 1; }` default with JS only *adding* the
    hidden state after confirming `IntersectionObserver` exists. That covers print, no-JS, and
    old browsers in one change without touching the animation itself.

================================================================
MAJOR
================================================================

M1. Icon-only fullscreen button has no accessible name for screen readers
    UI/stories.js:778-784:
        const fullscreenBtn = document.createElement('button');
        fullscreenBtn.textContent = '⛶';
        fullscreenBtn.title = 'Toggle Fullscreen';   // title only, no aria-label
    `title` is not reliably exposed to the accessibility tree the way `aria-label` is (and isn't
    reachable at all on touch devices). A screen-reader user hears "button" with no name, or at
    best the raw glyph. This is precisely the failure mode both charters call out by name
    ("fullscreen ... easy to ship without a label"). Same gap for the exit control created in
    `createFullscreenControls()` — check its button markup too (UI/stories.js:891+) for the same
    title-only pattern.
    Fix: `fullscreenBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Toggle fullscreen');` (and update the label on
    exit-state text/icon swap). One line.

M2. Mobile hamburger nav toggle is well under the 44x44 touch-target minimum
    UI/styles.css:124-131:
        .nav-toggle { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:5px; padding:4px; border:none; }
        .nav-toggle span { width:26px; height:3px; ... }
    Computed box: width ≈ 26 + 2×4(padding) = 34px; height ≈ (3×3px spans + 2×5px gaps) + 2×4px
    padding ≈ 19 + 8 = 27px. Actual tappable area ≈ 34×27px — about 60% of the 44×44 CSS-px
    minimum by area. This is the only way to open navigation on phones (`display:flex` only under
    `@media (max-width:768px)`, UI/styles.css:739), so it's a primary control, not a peripheral one.
    Fix: add `min-width:44px; min-height:44px;` and center the icon inside, or increase padding to
    ~9px on each side.

M3. Two `<h1>`s per page — page title competes with a `<h1>` site logo
    UI/surah-fatiha.html:29 `<h1 data-ar="أطفال الإسلام">Islamic Kids</h1>` (header logo) and
    UI/surah-fatiha.html:50 `<h1 data-ar="سورة الفاتحة">Surah Al-Fatiha</h1>` (page title) both
    exist on the same page; the same pattern repeats across every story/surah page. Screen-reader
    users jumping "by headings" land on the site name before the actual page subject, and the
    single-h1-per-page best practice (WCAG 2.4.6 / HTML5 outline guidance) is broken. Not a hard
    AA failure but a real navigation-quality issue for a repeat-visitor site with many pages.
    Fix: demote the logo to a `<p class="logo-text">`/`<span>` or `<div role="heading"
    aria-level="1">` only on the homepage, and keep it a plain link label everywhere else so each
    inner page has exactly one `<h1>`, the page subject.

M4. Decorative emoji are not hidden from assistive tech anywhere on the site
    `grep -c aria-hidden` across UI/stories.html, UI/surah-fatiha.html, UI/story-prophet-adam.html
    → 0 in all three. Every decorative glyph (📖 🌟 📿 ✨ 🩺 💬 💡 ⛶ ☪, etc.), including inside
    headings like `<h2 data-ar="...">🌟 Why is Surah Al-Fatiha Special?</h2>`
    (UI/surah-fatiha.html:67), is exposed to screen readers as literal announced content ("star
    star Why is..."). This is the exact concern flagged in the accessibility-auditor charter
    ("Emoji used decoratively should not be announced as content").
    Fix: wrap purely decorative emoji in `<span aria-hidden="true">🌟</span>` site-wide; keep them
    in `data-ar` too since bilingual.js swaps innerHTML wholesale (UI/bilingual.js:44-49) — the
    aria-hidden span needs to be replicated on both the English and Arabic markup for each node.

M5. `--gold` / `--gold-light` used as small-body-text/border color elsewhere and inherited by
    downstream failing pairs
    In addition to C1/C2: UI/styles.css:532 `border-color: var(--gold)` on a component whose
    text sits near it — borders at 3:1 are a lower bar (UI border contrast, not text), so this one
    is likely fine (border, not text) but flagging because `--gold` is used 2x as `color:` outside
    the two already-cited large-heading contexts across styles.css/surah.css per the hex census —
    worth a project-wide sweep for any additional small-text usage of `--gold`/`--gold-light`
    beyond the two found (`surah.css:264`, `surah.css:459`, `styles.css:229`). None of the 5
    remaining `var(--gold)`/`var(--gold-light)` occurrences found were on body-copy-sized text in
    this pass, but recommend a lint rule (see F1) so a future PR can't reintroduce it silently.

M6. `.lang-toggle-btn` and `.fullscreen-toggle` collide across stylesheets by page type
    `.lang-toggle-btn` is defined twice: UI/styles.css:858-874 (gold pill, ink text) and
    UI/stories.css:1460-1474 (translucent-white pill, white text). Load order is styles.css then
    the page sheet (per frontend-lead charter), so on any page that loads stories.css AFTER
    styles.css, the stories.css version wins for that page's toggle, and the styles.css version
    only applies on pages that don't load stories.css (surah pages). This is the same failure
    mode the frontend-lead charter names ("A toggle's styles lived in stories.css ... surah pages
    do not load [it], so the control was unstyled there") — here it's not unstyled but silently
    *inconsistent* branding/contrast between page types, and a future edit to one copy alone will
    silently diverge again. The stories.css copy (UI/stories.css:1460) has no `:focus-visible`
    rule of its own — it relies on the generic `button:focus-visible` in styles.css
    (UI/styles.css:821), which is fine functionally, but there's no `.lang-toggle-btn:focus-visible`
    override on story pages, so the focus ring there is the plain default outline rather than the
    styled amber one surah pages get (UI/styles.css:880-883). Minor inconsistency, real duplication
    risk.
    Fix: consolidate `.lang-toggle-btn` into styles.css only (it's a token-layer component, not
    page-type-specific); delete the stories.css copy or make it intentionally theme via a
    modifier class instead of a full redefinition.

================================================================
MINOR
================================================================

M7. lang/dir runtime switching is actually implemented correctly — verified, not a bug
    UI/bilingual.js:38-40 sets `root.lang`, `root.dir`, and `data-lang` together inside `apply()`,
    called both on init and on toggle. This is correct and avoids the "stale lang read in an
    English voice" failure the charter warns about. No action needed — noting as a pass since it
    was explicitly asked to be checked.

M8. `prefers-reduced-motion` is implemented once, correctly, but has a narrower blast radius than
    the animations on the page
    UI/styles.css:834-846 covers all `animation-duration`/`transition-duration`/`scroll-behavior`
    globally (via the `*` selector) plus `.fade-in { opacity: 1 !important; }` specifically — this
    does neutralize the confetti keyframes injected by UI/surah.js:145-153 (`@keyframes
    confettiFall`) since the universal-selector override with `!important` still wins regardless of
    which stylesheet declared the animation. Good. But it does NOT cover the scroll-reveal opacity
    on `.verse-card/.fact-card/.tip-card` (see C3) since those are set via inline style, not a
    class the reduced-motion block targets. Recommend folding a fix for C3 into this same rule so
    one change resolves both the print/no-JS risk and the reduced-motion gap.

M9. `--gold-text` #8A6220 vs `--gold` #C08A2E: correct fix exists, just under-applied
    Confirms the frontend-lead charter's own note that `--gold-text` was created for this exact
    reason. gold-text on parchment = 5.06:1 (PASS), gold-text on parchment-deep = 4.53:1 (PASS,
    just over 4.5). The fix for C1/C2 already exists as a token — this is a rollout gap, not a
    missing solution.

M10. surah.css has zero RTL mirror rules despite the page supporting live language switching
     `grep -c 'html\[lang="ar"\]' surah.css` → 0, vs. 34 LTR-assuming declarations
     (`text-align:left`/`margin-left`/`border-left`/`padding-left` combined) in the same file.
     Concretely: UI/surah.css:135 `border-left: 5px solid #1A8F80;` and UI/surah.css:139
     `border-left-color: #C08A2E;` on `.intro-card`/`.intro-card.highlight` — this accent border
     stays on the visual left edge even in Arabic/RTL mode (confirmed the toggle is live on this
     page: UI/surah-fatiha.html:212 already has a `dir="rtl"` node, and bilingual.js flips
     `html[dir]` at runtime). In RTL reading order the visual left is the trailing edge, so the
     "highlight" accent reads as an afterthought instead of a leading accent. Visual-only bug, not
     a WCAG failure, but breaks the RTL polish the frontend-lead charter is responsible for.
     Contrast: stories.css does this correctly — 34 `html[lang="ar"]` overrides exist there
     (including in interactive-story.css, story-styles.css) — so the pattern is known and just
     wasn't carried into surah.css.
     Fix: add `html[lang="ar"] .intro-card, html[lang="ar"] .intro-card.highlight { border-left:
     none; border-right: 5px solid ...; }` (2 rules) or better, refactor to a logical property:
     `border-inline-start` instead of `border-left`, which mirrors automatically with `dir` and
     needs zero override rules going forward.

M11. `.lang-toggle-btn` (styles.css version) is under the 44px touch-target height
     UI/styles.css:858-865: `padding: 0.4rem 1rem; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.4;` with a
     2px border. Computed height ≈ 14.4px(font) × 1.4(line-height) + 12.8px(padding) + 4px(border)
     ≈ 37px — about 84% of the 44px minimum. Width is comfortably over 44px so this is a
     height-only shortfall. Low severity (single-axis, close to threshold) but worth a
     `min-height: 44px;` addition alongside the M6 consolidation.

================================================================
Hardcoded-hex census (frontend-lead hard rule #1: tokens only in page-type sheets)
================================================================
Regex count of `#[0-9a-f]{3,8}` literals, page-type sheets only (styles.css excluded from the
"never hardcode" rule since it's the token layer, included below for reference):

  stories.css              141 occurrences / 31 unique hex values
  surah.css                 75 occurrences / 26 unique hex values
  interactive-story.css     70 occurrences / 28 unique hex values
  story-styles.css          39 occurrences / 23 unique hex values
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  page-type sheets total:  325 hardcoded hex occurrences / ~50 unique values
  styles.css (token layer): 40 occurrences (mostly legitimate — inside the --girih/--shamsa SVG
                             data-URIs which must be literal hex, plus var() alternates)

Worst offenders (repeated raw literals that already have a token equivalent and should be
`var(...)`  instead):
  #1A8F80  → 26x stories.css, 13x surah.css, 12x interactive-story.css, 4x story-styles.css
             (= --teal-500/--emerald-500 territory; 55 occurrences total, single highest offender)
  #C08A2E  → 11x stories.css, 6x surah.css, 3x interactive-story.css, 2x story-styles.css
             (= --gold, 22 occurrences — every one of these should be `var(--gold)`)
  #E6DCC8  → 14x stories.css, 3x surah.css, 2x interactive-story.css (= --slate-200)
  #1E9B92  → 11x stories.css, 4x surah.css (= --teal-400)
  #FBF6EA  → 10x stories.css, 2x surah.css, 6x interactive-story.css, 1x story-styles.css
             (= --parchment/--cream — the base background color reimplemented 19x)
  #A07223  → 7x stories.css, 3x surah.css, 1x story-styles.css (= --amber-600)
  #5C7370  → 7x stories.css, 4x surah.css, 5x interactive-story.css, 1x story-styles.css
             (= --slate-500)
  #7A5518  → 4x stories.css, 5x surah.css, 4x interactive-story.css, 2x story-styles.css — this
             hex has NO matching token in styles.css `:root` at all (closest is --gold-text
             #8A6220, a different value) — 15 occurrences of an entirely untokenized color.
  #ef4444 / #fee2e2 → interactive-story.css only, 2x each — this is the reserved red for
             wrong-answer feedback (frontend-lead exception #2), correctly NOT tokenized. Not a
             violation.
  #000 / #fff → low-value literals (2x each in styles.css/surah.css) for shadow rgba bases —
             cosmetic, not worth chasing.

This is the same 329-hardcoded-colors problem the frontend-lead charter says "was remapped once
already" — it appears to have regressed back to ~325 occurrences in the page-type sheets, with
#1A8F80 and #C08A2E as the two highest-value targets to convert to `var(--teal-500)`/
`var(--gold)` first (81 combined occurrences, would meaningfully cut the count).

================================================================
Verified fine / no action needed
================================================================
- Body-copy contrast: ink(#14383A) on parchment(#FBF6EA) = 11.75:1, excellent.
- cobalt(#24508C) on parchment = 7.49:1, PASS.
- quiz-option resting state text(#2E5B58) on white = 7.63:1, PASS.
- header white-on-turquoise-deep = 6.34:1, PASS.
- .fullscreen-toggle is 50x50px (UI/stories.css:1527-1533) — passes the 44x44 minimum, and has a
  correct RTL mirror (`right`→`left` swap at UI/stories.css:1553).
- Focus-visible rings exist and are reasonably broad: `a, button, .quiz-option, .nav-toggle` share
  one rule (UI/styles.css:820-823) plus a dedicated `.lang-toggle-btn:focus-visible`
  (UI/styles.css:880-883). Since `.fullscreen-toggle`/quiz options are real `<button>` elements,
  the generic `button:focus-visible` selector covers them even without a dedicated rule.
- Only one raw `<img>` tag exists across the entire UI/ tree (grep confirmed) — the site is
  emoji/CSS-illustration based, so missing alt text is a near-non-issue; did not find an
  unlabelled `<img>`.
- 360px width: `.nav-cluster`/`.lang-toggle-btn` have a dedicated `@media (max-width: 768px)`
  rule (UI/styles.css:959-966) that shrinks gap/padding/font-size — should not overflow at 360px.
- `quiz-option` computed touch-target size (1rem padding all sides + border, 2-col grid) comfortably
  clears 44x44 in both axes; not flagged.

================================================================
Priority order for a fix PR
================================================================
1. C3 (surah.js inline opacity, print/no-JS risk) — highest real-world impact given stated
   print-to-read use case, and cheapest fix (swap inline style for a class + one print rule).
2. C1 (arabic-full 1.86:1 on verse cards) — one-line token swap, fixes the actual Quran text.
3. C2 (hero-eyebrow 2.82:1) — one-line token swap.
4. M1 (fullscreen aria-label) — one line.
5. M2 (nav-toggle touch target) — a few lines of CSS.
6. M4 (aria-hidden on decorative emoji) — mechanical but touches every page; can be scripted.
7. M6/M11 (lang-toggle-btn consolidation + min-height) — do together, one PR.
8. M3 (double h1) — cosmetic/structural, low urgency.
9. M10 (surah.css RTL borders) — cosmetic, low urgency, but cheap if bundled with other surah.css
   edits.
10. Hardcoded-hex sweep — largest line-count, no urgency, best done as its own mechanical PR
    (find/replace #1A8F80→var(--teal-500), #C08A2E→var(--gold), etc.) with a visual diff check
    per frontend-lead's "verify in a real browser, screenshot before/after" method.
